    "result_text": "All 256 tests pass. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization on owner creation for the OfficeFloor `POST /api/owners` pipeline.\n\n**New files**\n- `AddressNormalizer.java` \u2014 shared utility: trims/collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-token abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`, tolerating a trailing `.`). Returns `null` when blank after normalization. So `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`.\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java` \u2014 pipeline step that mutates the built `Owner` in place with the normalized address, and enforces the required-field rule against the normalized form (throws a 400 when blank).\n- `InvalidAddressException.java` + `InvalidAddressExceptionHandler.java` + its escalation YAML \u2014 checked exception mapped to `400 Bad Request`, mirroring the existing telephone/email normalizers.\n\n**Changed files**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `normalizeAddress` step (under the create transaction) after email normalization and before the duplicate/household checks, so the stored and returned address is the normalized value and the household steps see it.\n- `RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold.java` and `AssignOwnerHousehold.java` \u2014 address comparisons (household duplicate detection and the shared `householdId` derivation) now canonicalize both sides via `AddressNormalizer.normalize`, replacing the old lowercase-only rule. Last-name comparison is unchanged. Javadocs updated to match.\n\n**Behaviour change:** the required-field check now rejects an address that is blank after normalization (400), and all address comparisons use the normalized form \u2014 exactly matching the new acceptance tests (`coreNormalizesWhitespaceAndCase`, `functionalityExpandsAbbreviations`, `errorRejectsBlankAfterNormalize`). Normalization is applied only on create (POST), not on update (PUT), per the spec. Existing controller/repository tests that don't assert the echoed address value remain green.",
